---
title: Risotto with datterini tomatoes, pecorino and mint
description: A really light risotto. There’s no butter in it – just olive oil and cheese – and the richness comes predominantly from the roast tomatoes
image: https://media.guim.co.uk/8b99e39014ddd974bec75ccac5a8c357e7d9c55e/173_0_4222_5278/1600.jpg
time required: 1 hour 35 minutes
cook time: 1 hour 20 minutes
course: Easy, Midweek, Lunch, Dinner
servings: 4
source: https://www.theguardian.com/food/2026/aug/19/risotto-datterini-tomatoes-amaretto-roast-peaches-recipe-chris-shaw
diet: Vegetarian, GlutenFree
author: Chris Shaw
prep time: 15 minutes
---

Roughly chop @shallots{4}(peeled), @celery{2 sticks}(trimmed), and @tomatoes{1}(vine) and put them in a #saucepan{}.

Cover with @water{2%litres} and bring to a boil over a high heat.

Turn down the heat and leave to simmer for ~{20%minutes}.

Meanwhile, heat the oven to 150°C. Put @tomatoes{500%g}(washed) in a roasting tray, season with @salt{} and drizzle with @olive oil{}.

Add @garlic{4%clove}(peeled) and roast for about ~{60%minutes}, until the tomatoes are jammy.

Bring a large, heavy-based pan to a medium heat.

Add a generous glug of @olive oil{}, followed by @shallots{2}(peeled and finely diced), @garlic{1%clove}(crushed), and a pinch of @salt{}.

Once the shallots are soft, add @risotto rice{350%g} and stir well to coat.

Add another pinch of @salt{}, then toast the rice for ~{1-2%minutes}, until the edges begin to look translucent.

Add @tomato puree{2%tbsp} and cook for ~{2-3%minutes}.

Strain the stock and discard the vegetable solids.

Add the stock to the rice one large ladleful at a time, stirring continuously and allowing each addition to be absorbed before adding the next.

After about ~{20%minutes}, when the rice is just cooked but still has a little bite, take the pan off the heat and stir through the roast @tomatoes{}, roast @garlic{}, @pecorino{150%g}(grated), and @mint leaves{30%g}(picked and finely chopped).

The tomatoes should break up nicely once incorporated into the hot rice.

Season to taste, finish with a generous glug of @olive oil{} if desired, and serve immediately.
